; -*- mode: lisp -*-
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; AUTHORED -- there is no upstream .bddl for this task.
;
; provenance.tier: reproduction
; source-of-record: robosimstudio/tasks/cable_route/suite.py + evaluator.py
; upstream:         newton_tasks/sim/yam_cable_sim.py, at its defaults.
;
; WHAT MAKES THIS ONE INTERESTING. It is the first document about a CHAIN --
; a 0.91 m rod of 28 capsules -- and the first whose start state is a robot
; posture rather than an object pose. The second hand is closed on the cable
; before the first step, and stays closed for the episode; without it the whole
; rod follows the working hand around the peg instead of winding onto it.
;
; The reference ships NO success predicate for either cable variant -- a NaN
; guard is its only assertion -- so both goals are designed. What is transcribed
; is the scene, the physics and the script; what is committed to here is what
; counts as done.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(define (problem WIND_THE_CABLE_AROUND_THE_FRONT_PEG)

  (:suite cable_route)

  (:language wind the cable around the front peg while the other hand anchors it)

  ; The cell, the robot, the arrangement -- a named one, never a solver config
  ; -- and the episode are the suite's; only what this variant changes is here.
  (:staging
    ; Fixed rather than aimed: both views look at the middle of the working
    ; area, a constant of the bench.
    (:cameras cable_overhead_corner@v1)
    ; The script is 60 settle + ~790 steps; a graph blocks on every robot.* call
    ; and needs several times that.
    (:max-steps 5000)
  )

  ; `cable_rod` -- the reference's own 0.91 m rod with its anchor feature --
  ; laid along the reference's line with the second hand on it. The type is the
  ; placed record: `wrap_cable` is `cable_rod`'s declaration with the factory
  ; that puts it there, minted in assets/layout.py.
  (:objects
    cable - wrap_cable
  )

  (:fixtures
    ; Three pegs stand on the bench and the task uses one of them. The other two
    ; are declared because they are THERE -- the rod can foul them, and a
    ; document that named only the peg it wraps would describe a different
    ; scene than the one that runs. Each type is `cable_peg` at one of the
    ; reference's three posts; `peg0.axis` below is that declaration's.
    peg0 - wrap_peg0
    peg1 - wrap_peg1
    peg2 - wrap_peg2
  )

  ; No regions. Everything this variant scores on is measured against the peg's
  ; own declared axis or against the rod itself, which is why the same goal aimed
  ; at a bollard, a spool or a doorknob needs no new numbers.

  ; -------------------------------------------------------------------------
  ; THE ANCHOR, and it is an init constraint rather than a goal.
  ;
  ; `cable.anchor` is an `anchor` feature on the rod: hold me at s = 0.6361 --
  ; the reference's own y = 0.12, in the rod's own parameterisation, which is
  ; the form that survives a table of a different height -- at a 10 mm finger
  ; command, and never let go. The kind's contract says an anchor
  ; "shapes the state an episode begins in and must be verified to have taken
  ; hold before the episode counts as started", and `anchor-held` is that
  ; verification -- a latch flag the entity publishes, because a kinematic
  ; coupling written into a device array is not something a pose or a contact
  ; can be asked about.
  ;
  ; ARRANGING it is the loader's, not this file's: the compiler owns the scene
  ; lifecycle and the order is exact -- solve the support arm's full pose above
  ; the grasp, chain a second solve down onto it, write the result into both
  ; states AND the control targets, run FK, copy body_q across, then latch. Get
  ; the order wrong and the cable starts somewhere subtly else; get the targets
  ; wrong and the arm spends the settle climbing away from its own pinch,
  ; sweeping the rod 150 mm across the table on the way out.
  (:init
    (anchor-held cable)
  )

  ; -------------------------------------------------------------------------
  ; THREE CLAUSES, and NOTHING LATCHES.
  ;
  ; (winding-turns cable peg0.axis) >= 0.5
  ;     Walk the chain; for each consecutive pair of segments within 0.10 m of
  ;     the axis, accumulate the SIGNED angle their midpoints subtend about it.
  ;     The magnitude of that sum is how many turns are actually on the peg -- a
  ;     hank dumped beside it scores zero, a figure-eight cancels itself, and
  ;     those two properties are what "wrapped" has to mean.
  ;
  ;     THE GATE CARRIES ITS CALIBRATION, and this is the one threshold in the
  ;     repo that earned its number rather than being chosen: every wrap the
  ;     shipped routine or graph produces retains 0.55-0.96 turns after release
  ;     (run-to-run variance is real -- the runtime thread's timing changes the
  ;     drag dynamics lap to lap), while every non-wrap outcome ever traced --
  ;     drag-pasts, slipped grasps, a two-lap coil springing open -- measures
  ;     0.25 or less. Half a turn sits in the middle of that valley: physically
  ;     it means the rod is bent around the peg and staying there, which a
  ;     drag-past cannot fake by a factor of three. The atom records the same
  ;     two populations as data; see AtomCalibration on `winding_turns`.
  ;
  ;     Of the SCRIPT's 3 laps, not of its 2: friction takes some of every lap,
  ;     and demanding the scripted number would measure slack, not wrapping.
  ;
  ; (tcp-distance cable) > 0.05
  ;     Let go. Read against the NEAREST segment, because release is a claim
  ;     about the whole rod: a hand off the middle but still closed on one end
  ;     has not released. There is no jaw-stall signal to read here -- the hold
  ;     is a kinematic latch and the fingers never carry the load -- which is
  ;     why this is `tcp-distance` and not `gripped`.
  ;
  ; (speed cable) <= 0.05
  ;     Still moving is not wound. The mean of the segment SPEEDS, not the speed
  ;     of the mean: a rod mid-drag has segments going opposite ways and the
  ;     centroid barely moves, so the vector average would call it at rest.
  ;
  ; NOTHING LATCHES, and that is the shipped reading rather than the obvious
  ; one. A `once` here would score a wrap that sprang open two seconds later --
  ; and springing open is precisely the failure the third lap was added to
  ; prevent, so latching would hide the thing the task is measuring. The
  ; evaluator this replaces says it in one line: "Neither condition latches: a
  ; placement dragged off the mark or a wrap that unwinds after release is
  ; scored where it *is*."
  (:goal
    (And
      (>= (winding-turns cable peg0.axis) 0.5)
      (> (tcp-distance cable) 0.05)
      (<= (speed cable) 0.05)
    )
  )

  ; -------------------------------------------------------------------------
  ; Progress toward the winding gate, clipped.
  ;
  ; NOT the satisfied share, which is what the other cable variant uses and what
  ; a reader assumes. The shipped evaluator computes `clip(turns / gate)` and
  ; falls back to 1.0 once all three flags hold -- which is the same number,
  ; since all three holding requires turns >= gate and the clip is already 1.0
  ; there. The special case was redundant; this is what it computed.
  (:reward (progress (winding-turns cable peg0.axis) (:gate 0.5)))

  ; -------------------------------------------------------------------------
  ; Recorded every step, gating nothing.
  ;
  ; `grasped_ever` in the Python's metrics, restated: the hand was on the rod at
  ; some point. It is what separates a grasp that slipped from one that never
  ; happened, and the two look identical in a final-state reading.
  (:observe
    (once (<= (tcp-distance cable) 0.05))
    (straightness cable)
  )

  ; -------------------------------------------------------------------------
  (:artifacts
    (:routine "routine:RoutineCableRoute")
    (:graph gt "graphs/gt")
  )

  ; -------------------------------------------------------------------------
  ; Inputs to the ARTIFACT, not statements about the world. The keyframe tables
  ; live with the routine; these are the numbers that pick which table and where
  ; to aim it, and they are fenced off here because the moment routine
  ; parameters mix with goal parameters the untyped side channel this port
  ; exists to delete has been reinvented under a new name.
  (:policy-params
    (mode wrap)
    ; Where the script reaches for the rod [m]. The reference's own bend, and a
    ; coordinate rather than an arc length because that is what it is: an input
    ; to a scripted table, not a statement about the world -- which is exactly
    ; the line this section draws. The routine re-detects the nearest segment
    ; live before it closes, so this aims the search rather than fixing the
    ; grasp, and the ring's start angle is struck from it.
    (grasp-y -0.18)
    (routine-solves true)
  )

  (:provenance
    (:tier reproduction)
    (:source "newton_tasks/sim/yam_cable_sim.py at defaults")
    (:note "28-capsule add_rod cable, SolverCoupledProxy(mjc+vbd), the reference's own
            keyframe scripts. The reference ships no success condition -- the winding
            measure and its gate are designed here, and the gate is calibrated into a
            measured separation rather than chosen. The script runs 3 laps where the
            reference scripts 2: one lap around a 13 mm peg has no capstan margin
            against this rod's bend energy and springs back to a quarter turn within
            two seconds of release.")
  )
)
